// CHAPTER 3: Study Skills (Term 1)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Study Skills\n\nEffective studying is a skill that can be learned and improved. Understanding your study style, using the right learning strategies, and preparing for exams will help you perform at your best in Grade 11 and beyond.\n\n### Study Styles and Learning Preferences\n\nEvery learner processes information differently. Understanding your preferred style helps you study more efficiently:\n\n| Style | How you learn best | Effective strategies |\n|-------|-------------------|---------------------|\n| **Visual** | Through seeing (diagrams, charts, videos) | Mind maps, colour-coded notes, flowcharts, posters, watching educational videos |\n| **Auditory** | Through hearing (lectures, discussions) | Reading aloud, recording notes and replaying, group discussions, verbal explanations |\n| **Reading/Writing** | Through text (reading, note-taking) | Summarising, rewriting notes, making lists, reading multiple sources |\n| **Kinaesthetic** | Through doing (hands-on, movement) | Practising problems, role-play, building models, walking while revising |'),
  t(2, '### Learning Strategies for Grade 11\n\nGrade 11 introduces more complex content across all subjects. These strategies will help:\n\n**Active recall:** After reading a section, close the book and write down everything you remember. This forces your brain to retrieve information, strengthening memory.\n\n**Spaced repetition:** Review material at increasing intervals. Study a topic today, review it after 2 days, then after 5 days, then after 10 days. This is far more effective than cramming.\n\n**The Pomodoro Technique:** Study for 25 minutes, take a 5-minute break. After 4 cycles, take a longer 20-minute break. This prevents burnout and maintains focus.\n\n**Elaborative interrogation:** Ask "why?" and "how?" about everything you study. Do not just memorise facts — understand the reasoning behind them.\n\n**Interleaving:** Mix different topics or subjects in one study session rather than studying one topic for hours. This improves your ability to distinguish between concepts.'),
  q(3, 'Ayanda studies Accounting for 4 hours straight, then does the same for Physical Science the next day. Which study technique would improve her approach?',
    ['Interleaving — mixing different subjects in one session', 'Studying only one subject per week', 'Reading through notes once before the exam', 'Highlighting every line in the textbook'], 0,
    'Interleaving involves alternating between topics or subjects during a study session. This helps the brain make connections and improves long-term retention compared to massed practice of one subject.'),
  t(4, '### Exam Writing Skills\n\nKnowing the content is only half the battle. You also need to know how to present your answers effectively:\n\n**Understanding command words:**\n\n| Command word | What it requires |\n|-------------|------------------|\n| **Define** | Give the exact meaning of the term |\n| **Identify** | Name or point out specific items |\n| **Describe** | Give a detailed account of features or characteristics |\n| **Explain** | Give reasons or make the meaning clear |\n| **Discuss** | Present different aspects, arguments for and against |\n| **Evaluate** | Make a judgement using evidence, weigh pros and cons |\n| **Analyse** | Break down into parts and examine each |\n| **Compare** | Show similarities AND differences |\n| **Distinguish** | Show only the differences |'),
  t(5, '### Exam Writing Tips\n\n**Before the exam:**\n- Read through the entire paper before answering\n- Allocate time per section based on mark allocation\n- Start with sections you feel most confident about\n\n**During the exam:**\n- Read each question twice before answering\n- Underline key command words\n- Use the mark allocation as a guide: 4 marks = 4 distinct points\n- Write in full sentences for discussion questions\n- Use bullet points for listing or identifying\n- Answer ALL questions — never leave a question blank\n\n**After the exam:**\n- Review your answers if time allows\n- Check that you have answered all questions\n- Ensure your name and exam number are on every page'),
  fb(6, 'The study technique of testing yourself on material rather than re-reading is called ___. Reviewing material at increasing intervals is called ___ repetition.',
    ['active recall', 'spaced'],
    'Active recall strengthens memory by forcing retrieval. Spaced repetition distributes study over time, which is more effective than cramming.'),
  t(7, '### Creating an Effective Study Plan\n\nA study plan helps you manage your time, cover all subjects, and reduce stress:\n\n**Steps to create your study plan:**\n1. **List all subjects** and identify which need the most attention\n2. **Check your exam timetable** and work backwards from each exam date\n3. **Allocate time blocks** — study your most difficult subjects during your peak focus time (morning for most people)\n4. **Include breaks** — the brain needs rest to consolidate learning\n5. **Set specific goals** for each session ("Complete Chapter 4 summary" not just "Study Life Orientation")\n6. **Build in flexibility** — life happens; allow catch-up time\n7. **Review weekly** — adjust the plan based on your progress\n\n**Study environment:**\n- Find a quiet, well-lit space\n- Remove distractions (phone on silent, social media logged out)\n- Have all materials ready before you start\n- If you do not have a quiet space at home, use the school library or a community library'),
  q(8, 'Bongani sets a study goal of "Study for 3 hours tonight." Why is this NOT an effective study goal?',
    ['It is vague and does not specify what content to cover or what outcome to achieve', 'Three hours is too short for effective studying', 'Goals should only focus on one subject', 'Study goals are unnecessary for effective studying'], 0,
    'Effective study goals are specific and measurable, such as "Complete past paper 2 on healthy lifestyles and score above 70%." Vague goals like "study for 3 hours" lead to unfocused studying.'),
  t(9, '### Dealing with Study Challenges\n\nCommon challenges Grade 11 learners face and how to overcome them:\n\n**Procrastination:**\n- Break large tasks into small, manageable steps\n- Use the "two-minute rule" — if it takes less than two minutes, do it now\n- Reward yourself after completing tasks\n\n**Lack of motivation:**\n- Connect your studies to your career goals\n- Study with a motivated friend or study group\n- Visualise your success\n\n**Distractions:**\n- Use apps that block social media during study time\n- Tell family and friends when you are studying so they respect your time\n- If home is too noisy, find an alternative study space\n\n**Anxiety about exams:**\n- Preparation is the best antidote to anxiety\n- Practise deep breathing exercises before and during exams\n- Remember: one exam does not define your worth\n- Speak to a teacher or counsellor if anxiety is overwhelming'),
  q(10, 'Which combination of study strategies would be MOST effective for preparing for a Grade 11 Life Orientation test?',
    ['Active recall with spaced repetition, practising past papers, and using mind maps for visual topics', 'Reading through notes once the night before the test', 'Highlighting every paragraph and hoping to remember it', 'Studying for 8 hours straight without breaks'], 0,
    'The combination of active recall, spaced repetition, past papers, and visual aids engages multiple learning strategies and is supported by research as the most effective approach to studying.'),
];

