// CHAPTER 4: Careers and Career Choices (Term 1)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Careers and Career Choices\n\nGrade 11 is a critical year for career planning. The subjects you are taking now, the skills you are developing, and the decisions you make will shape your post-school path. This chapter covers competencies, career advertisements, CV writing, job interviews, and exploring your career interests.\n\n### Competencies, Abilities, and Ethics in the Workplace\n\n**Competencies** are the skills, knowledge, and behaviours needed to perform effectively in a job:\n\n| Competency type | Description | Examples |\n|----------------|-------------|----------|\n| **Technical skills** | Job-specific knowledge and abilities | Computer literacy, accounting, welding, teaching methodology |\n| **Soft skills** | Interpersonal and communication abilities | Teamwork, leadership, time management, conflict resolution |\n| **Emotional intelligence** | Ability to manage your own emotions and understand others | Empathy, self-awareness, stress management |\n| **Ethical competence** | Acting with integrity and professionalism | Honesty, accountability, respect for diversity |'),
  t(2, '### Workplace Ethics\n\nEthics refers to the moral principles that guide behaviour in the workplace:\n\n**Key ethical principles:**\n- **Integrity:** Being honest and having strong moral principles\n- **Accountability:** Taking responsibility for your actions\n- **Confidentiality:** Protecting private information\n- **Respect:** Treating colleagues, clients, and the public with dignity\n- **Fairness:** Treating everyone equitably regardless of race, gender, or background\n\n**Unethical behaviour in the workplace includes:**\n- Fraud and corruption (stealing company resources, accepting bribes)\n- Discrimination and harassment\n- Nepotism (hiring family members regardless of qualifications)\n- Plagiarism and dishonesty\n- Arriving late, leaving early, or not doing your work\n\nIn South Africa, the Companies Act and King IV Report on Corporate Governance set standards for ethical business conduct.'),
  q(3, 'A company hires the manager\'s nephew for a position even though more qualified candidates applied. This is an example of:',
    ['Nepotism', 'Affirmative action', 'Fair recruitment', 'Employment equity'], 0,
    'Nepotism is the practice of favouring family members or friends in hiring decisions regardless of their qualifications. This is unethical and differs from affirmative action, which aims to correct historical imbalances.'),
  t(4, '### Reading and Responding to Career Advertisements\n\nJob advertisements contain key information you must understand:\n\n**Typical components of a job advert:**\n- **Job title:** The name of the position\n- **Company name and description:** Who the employer is\n- **Key responsibilities:** What the job involves\n- **Requirements:** Qualifications, experience, and skills needed\n- **Remuneration:** Salary or salary range (sometimes listed as "market-related")\n- **Application instructions:** How to apply, deadline, required documents\n- **Contact details:** Where to send your application\n\n**Reading between the lines:**\n- "Self-starter" = you must be able to work independently\n- "Team player" = collaboration is essential\n- "Deadline-driven" = the job involves pressure and strict timelines\n- "Market-related salary" = the salary is competitive but not disclosed'),
  t(5, '### CV Writing (Curriculum Vitae)\n\nYour CV is your first impression on a potential employer. It must be professional, clear, and honest.\n\n**Structure of a CV:**\n\n1. **Personal details:** Full name, contact number, email address, physical address\n2. **Personal statement/Profile:** 2-3 sentences summarising who you are and what you offer\n3. **Education:** Schools attended, highest grade completed, subjects (list from most recent)\n4. **Work experience:** Any part-time work, volunteering, or learnerships (list from most recent)\n5. **Skills:** Computer skills, languages spoken, driver\'s licence\n6. **Achievements:** Awards, leadership positions, sports achievements\n7. **References:** 2-3 people who can vouch for your character and abilities (ask permission first)\n\n**CV tips:**\n- Keep it to 2 pages maximum\n- Use a professional email address (not coolguy123@email.com)\n- Proofread carefully — spelling errors create a bad impression\n- Tailor your CV to each job you apply for\n- Never lie on your CV — it will catch up with you'),
  fb(6, 'A document summarising your qualifications, skills, and experience for job applications is called a ___. Hiring family members regardless of qualifications is called ___.',
    ['CV', 'nepotism'],
    'A CV (curriculum vitae) is a professional document used in job applications. Nepotism is the unethical practice of favouring relatives in hiring decisions.'),
  t(7, '### Job Interview Skills\n\nAn interview is your opportunity to make a personal impression and demonstrate why you are the right candidate:\n\n**Before the interview:**\n- Research the company thoroughly\n- Prepare answers to common questions:\n  - "Tell me about yourself"\n  - "Why do you want this position?"\n  - "What are your strengths and weaknesses?"\n  - "Where do you see yourself in five years?"\n- Plan your outfit (professional, neat, appropriate)\n- Know the location and arrive 10-15 minutes early\n\n**During the interview:**\n- Greet the interviewer with a firm handshake and eye contact\n- Speak clearly and confidently\n- Listen carefully to questions and answer specifically\n- Use examples to illustrate your points\n- Ask thoughtful questions about the role\n\n**After the interview:**\n- Thank the interviewer for their time\n- Follow up with a brief thank-you email if possible\n- Reflect on what went well and what you could improve'),
  q(8, 'Zanele has a job interview tomorrow. Which of the following should she do to prepare?',
    ['Research the company and practise answers to common interview questions', 'Arrive 30 minutes late to appear relaxed and casual', 'Make up qualifications to impress the interviewer', 'Spend the evening on social media and hope for the best'], 0,
    'Thorough preparation — including company research, practising answers, and planning logistics — is the key to a successful interview. Arriving late, lying, or failing to prepare are all likely to result in rejection.'),
  t(9, '### Exploring Career Interests\n\nChoosing a career is one of the most important decisions you will make. It should align with your interests, abilities, values, and personality:\n\n**Self-assessment questions:**\n- What subjects do I enjoy most? What am I good at?\n- What activities make me lose track of time?\n- Do I prefer working with people, data, things, or ideas?\n- Do I want to work indoors or outdoors?\n- Is salary, job satisfaction, or work-life balance most important to me?\n\n**Career exploration resources in South Africa:**\n- National Career Advice Portal (NCAP): www.careerhelp.org.za\n- SAQA (South African Qualifications Authority): www.saqa.org.za\n- Department of Higher Education and Training: www.dhet.gov.za\n- University open days and career expos\n- Job shadowing and mentoring programmes\n- PACE Career Centre\n\n**Post-school options:**\n- University (bachelor\'s degree — requires a bachelor\'s pass in NSC)\n- University of Technology (diplomas and BTech degrees)\n- TVET Colleges (vocational and technical qualifications)\n- Learnerships and apprenticeships (earn while you learn)\n- Entrepreneurship (starting your own business)'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following is the BEST approach to choosing a career?',
    ['Assessing your interests, abilities, and values, then researching careers that match', 'Choosing the career with the highest salary regardless of your interests', 'Following exactly what your parents want you to do', 'Waiting until after matric to think about it'], 0,
    'A well-informed career choice considers your personal interests, strengths, values, and personality alongside practical factors like job availability and earning potential. Starting early gives you time to explore and prepare.'),
];

