// CHAPTER 6: Social and Environmental Responsibility (Term 2)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Social and Environmental Responsibility\n\nAs citizens of South Africa, we have a responsibility to contribute to our communities and protect our environment. This chapter explores the role of government, community services, environmental programmes, and how to create a personal mission and vision statement.\n\n### Government Responsibilities\n\nSouth Africa has three levels of government, each with distinct responsibilities:\n\n| Level | Structure | Key responsibilities |\n|-------|----------|---------------------|\n| **National** | Parliament, President, Cabinet | Defence, foreign affairs, national health and education policy, SAPS, SARS, social grants |\n| **Provincial** | Premier, MECs, Provincial Legislature | Provincial roads, hospitals, schools, housing, agriculture, provincial planning |\n| **Local** | Municipalities (metros, districts, local) | Water supply, electricity, refuse removal, local roads, sanitation, community facilities |'),
  t(2, '### How Government Serves Communities\n\nGovernment delivers services through various mechanisms:\n\n**Social grants:** SASSA administers grants including the Child Support Grant (R530/month), Old Age Pension, Disability Grant, and Foster Care Grant. Over 18 million South Africans receive social grants.\n\n**Public healthcare:** Government clinics and hospitals provide free or subsidised healthcare. The National Health Insurance (NHI) aims to provide universal health coverage.\n\n**Education:** Public schools, TVET colleges, and universities receive government funding. No-fee schools serve the poorest communities. NSFAS provides funding for higher education.\n\n**Housing:** The RDP (Reconstruction and Development Programme) housing programme provides subsidised housing to qualifying citizens.\n\n**Challenges:**\n- Service delivery protests occur when communities feel government is not meeting their needs\n- Corruption diverts resources from service delivery\n- Infrastructure backlogs, especially in rural areas and informal settlements\n- Load-shedding reflects failures in energy planning and maintenance'),
  q(3, 'Which level of government is responsible for providing water supply and refuse removal to communities?',
    ['Local government (municipalities)', 'National government', 'Provincial government', 'All three levels equally'], 0,
    'Local government (municipalities) is responsible for basic services including water supply, electricity distribution, refuse removal, sanitation, and local roads. These are listed in Schedule 5B of the Constitution.'),
  t(4, '### Community Services\n\nCommunity services are organised efforts to address social needs and improve quality of life:\n\n**Types of community services in SA:**\n- **Non-governmental organisations (NGOs):** Organisations like Gift of the Givers, Habitat for Humanity SA, and local soup kitchens that provide aid and support\n- **Faith-based organisations:** Churches, mosques, and temples that run feeding schemes, youth programmes, and shelters\n- **Community policing forums (CPFs):** Partnerships between SAPS and community members to address crime\n- **Volunteer programmes:** Organisations that coordinate volunteers for community projects\n- **Stokvels:** Traditional savings groups that pool resources for community members\n\n**The role of youth in community service:**\n- Volunteering builds skills, empathy, and a sense of purpose\n- Community service can be included on your CV\n- Many universities and bursaries value community involvement\n- Young people bring energy, creativity, and fresh perspectives to community challenges'),
  t(5, '### Environmental Programmes and Responsibility\n\nSouth Africa faces significant environmental challenges:\n\n**Key environmental issues:**\n- **Water scarcity:** SA is a water-scarce country; droughts and pollution threaten water security\n- **Air pollution:** Industrial emissions, vehicle exhaust, and burning of fossil fuels\n- **Land degradation:** Overgrazing, deforestation, mining, and urbanisation\n- **Waste management:** Illegal dumping, inadequate recycling infrastructure\n- **Climate change:** Rising temperatures, more extreme weather events, sea-level rise\n- **Biodiversity loss:** Poaching, habitat destruction, invasive species\n\n**Environmental programmes in SA:**\n- Working for Water: Removes invasive alien plants and creates jobs\n- Working on Fire: Manages wildfire risk and provides employment\n- Expanded Public Works Programme (EPWP): Green economy projects\n- National Recycling Forum: Promotes recycling and waste reduction\n- SA National Parks (SANParks): Manages protected areas and biodiversity'),
  fb(6, 'The government agency that administers social grants in South Africa is called ___. South Africa has ___ levels of government.',
    ['SASSA', 'three'],
    'SASSA (South African Social Security Agency) administers social grants. South Africa has three levels of government: national, provincial, and local.'),
  t(7, '### Your Environmental Responsibility\n\nAs an individual, you can make a difference:\n\n**Reduce, Reuse, Recycle:**\n- Reduce water and electricity consumption\n- Reuse containers, bags, and materials\n- Separate waste for recycling (paper, plastic, glass, metal)\n\n**At school:**\n- Start or join an environmental club\n- Organise clean-up campaigns\n- Create a school vegetable garden\n- Reduce paper waste — use both sides\n\n**In your community:**\n- Report illegal dumping to your municipality\n- Participate in river and beach clean-ups\n- Plant trees and indigenous plants\n- Support local food gardens\n\n**Advocacy:**\n- Raise awareness about environmental issues on social media\n- Attend public participation processes on environmental decisions\n- Support organisations fighting for environmental justice\n- Section 24 of the Bill of Rights guarantees the right to an environment that is not harmful to health or well-being'),
  q(8, 'Section 24 of the South African Bill of Rights protects which of the following?',
    ['The right to an environment that is not harmful to health or well-being', 'The right to free housing for all citizens', 'The right to unlimited use of natural resources', 'The right to ignore environmental regulations'], 0,
    'Section 24 guarantees everyone the right to an environment that is not harmful to their health or well-being and requires the state to take measures to protect the environment for present and future generations.'),
  t(9, '### Personal Mission and Vision Statement\n\nA personal mission and vision statement helps you define your purpose and goals:\n\n**Vision statement:** Describes where you want to be in the future. It is aspirational and forward-looking.\n- Example: "I envision a career in environmental law where I can protect South Africa\'s natural heritage and ensure environmental justice for all communities."\n\n**Mission statement:** Describes what you do NOW to work toward your vision. It is action-oriented.\n- Example: "I commit to studying diligently, volunteering in community environmental projects, and developing my leadership skills to prepare for a career in environmental law."\n\n**How to write yours:**\n1. Reflect on your values: What matters most to you?\n2. Identify your strengths: What are you good at?\n3. Consider your passions: What makes you feel alive?\n4. Think about impact: How do you want to contribute to society?\n5. Write it down: Keep it concise, personal, and meaningful\n6. Review regularly: Your mission and vision may evolve as you grow'),
  q(10, 'What is the key difference between a personal vision statement and a personal mission statement?',
    ['A vision statement describes your future aspirations; a mission statement describes what you do now to achieve them', 'They are the same thing with different names', 'A vision statement is for companies only, not individuals', 'A mission statement is about money; a vision statement is about happiness'], 0,
    'A vision statement is future-oriented (where you want to be), while a mission statement is present-oriented (what you are doing now to get there). Both are important for personal goal-setting.'),
];

