// CHAPTER 7: Lifestyle Diseases and Health (Term 3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Lifestyle Diseases and Health\n\nThis chapter takes a deeper look at the human factors causing ill-health and the major lifestyle diseases affecting South Africans. Understanding these factors and diseases empowers you to make healthier choices and support those affected.\n\n### Human Factors Causing Ill-Health\n\n**Poor eating habits:**\n- Excessive consumption of processed foods high in sugar, salt, and unhealthy fats\n- Skipping meals, especially breakfast\n- Overeating or undereating\n- Reliance on fast food and sugary drinks\n- In SA, food insecurity in some communities leads to malnutrition, while urbanisation drives overconsumption of processed foods\n\n**Smoking and tobacco use:**\n- Cigarettes contain over 7 000 chemicals, including at least 70 known carcinogens\n- Smoking damages the lungs, heart, blood vessels, and nearly every organ\n- Secondhand smoke is also harmful, especially to children\n- E-cigarettes/vaping are not safe alternatives — they contain nicotine and harmful chemicals\n- In SA, the Tobacco Products Control Act regulates tobacco advertising and smoking in public places'),
  t(2, '### Substance Abuse\n\nSubstance abuse is a major cause of ill-health in South Africa:\n\n| Substance | Health effects | SA context |\n|-----------|---------------|------------|\n| **Alcohol** | Liver disease, brain damage, cardiovascular disease, foetal alcohol spectrum disorder (FASD) | SA has one of the highest rates of hazardous drinking globally; FASD rates in some SA regions are the highest in the world |\n| **Cannabis (dagga)** | Impaired memory, reduced motivation, respiratory issues, mental health problems | Legalised for private use (2018) but still harmful, especially for developing teenage brains |\n| **Methamphetamine (tik)** | Severe dental damage, psychosis, heart problems, extreme weight loss | Major problem in Western Cape and increasingly in other provinces |\n| **Nyaope/whoonga** | Severe addiction, organ damage, HIV risk from shared needles | Prevalent in Gauteng and KwaZulu-Natal |\n| **Prescription drug abuse** | Dependency, organ damage, overdose | Codeine-containing medications are commonly abused |'),
  q(3, 'Which of the following statements about e-cigarettes (vaping) is TRUE?',
    ['They contain nicotine and harmful chemicals and are not a safe alternative to smoking', 'They are completely safe because they do not produce smoke', 'They are recommended by doctors as a healthy habit', 'They contain no addictive substances'], 0,
    'E-cigarettes contain nicotine (which is addictive) and produce aerosols with harmful chemicals. They are not a safe alternative to traditional cigarettes, despite being marketed as such.'),
  t(4, '### Unsafe Sexual Behaviour\n\nUnsafe sexual behaviour is a significant health risk, particularly in South Africa:\n\n**Risks include:**\n- HIV/AIDS infection — SA has the largest HIV epidemic in the world (approximately 8 million people living with HIV)\n- Sexually transmitted infections (STIs): chlamydia, gonorrhoea, syphilis, herpes, HPV\n- Unintended pregnancy and its social and educational consequences\n- Emotional and psychological harm\n\n**Safe sexual behaviour:**\n- Abstinence is the only 100% effective method of preventing STIs and pregnancy\n- Consistent and correct condom use significantly reduces risk\n- Getting tested regularly for STIs and HIV\n- Being in a mutually faithful relationship with a partner who has tested negative\n- Male medical circumcision reduces HIV risk by approximately 60%\n- PrEP (pre-exposure prophylaxis) is available for people at high risk of HIV\n\n**SA resources:**\n- Free condoms are available at government clinics and many public places\n- loveLife provides youth-friendly sexual health services and education\n- Centres for Disease Control and Prevention supports HIV prevention programmes in SA'),
  fb(5, 'South Africa has the largest ___ epidemic in the world. The only 100% effective method of preventing STIs and pregnancy is ___.',
    ['HIV', 'abstinence'],
    'South Africa has approximately 8 million people living with HIV, the largest epidemic globally. While abstinence is the only fully effective prevention method, consistent condom use significantly reduces risk.'),
  t(6, '### Cancer\n\nCancer occurs when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably and can invade other tissues:\n\n**Common cancers in SA:**\n- Cervical cancer (leading cancer among SA women — largely preventable through HPV vaccination and screening)\n- Breast cancer\n- Prostate cancer (most common cancer among SA men)\n- Lung cancer (strongly linked to smoking)\n- Kaposi sarcoma (linked to HIV/AIDS)\n\n**Risk factors:**\n- Smoking (lung, throat, bladder, and other cancers)\n- Excessive alcohol (liver, breast, oesophageal cancers)\n- Obesity and poor diet\n- Excessive sun exposure without protection (skin cancer)\n- HPV infection (cervical and other cancers)\n- Genetic factors\n\n**Prevention:**\n- HPV vaccination for girls (available free in SA public schools)\n- Regular screening (Pap smears, breast self-examinations, prostate checks)\n- Avoid tobacco and limit alcohol\n- Maintain a healthy weight and eat a balanced diet\n- Use sunscreen and avoid excessive sun exposure\n- CANSA (Cancer Association of South Africa) provides education and support: 0800 22 66 22'),
  t(7, '### Tuberculosis (TB)\n\nTB is both an infectious disease and a disease worsened by lifestyle factors:\n\n**Key facts about TB in SA:**\n- SA has one of the highest TB rates in the world\n- TB and HIV form a deadly combination — people with HIV are much more likely to develop active TB\n- Drug-resistant TB (MDR-TB and XDR-TB) is a growing concern\n- TB thrives in overcrowded, poorly ventilated conditions\n\n**Lifestyle factors that increase TB risk:**\n- Smoking damages the lungs and weakens defences against TB\n- Alcohol abuse weakens the immune system\n- Malnutrition reduces the body\'s ability to fight infection\n- HIV infection severely compromises immunity\n\n**Prevention and treatment:**\n- Complete the full course of TB medication (6-9 months) — stopping early creates drug resistance\n- Good ventilation in homes and public spaces\n- Covering mouth when coughing, proper hand hygiene\n- BCG vaccination given at birth provides some protection\n- Free TB treatment is available at all government clinics in SA'),
  q(8, 'Why is it critical to complete the full course of TB medication, even after symptoms improve?',
    ['Stopping early allows surviving bacteria to develop drug resistance', 'The medication tastes good and should be enjoyed', 'Symptoms improving means the disease is getting worse', 'TB medication has no side effects and is harmless to take indefinitely'], 0,
    'Incomplete TB treatment kills weaker bacteria but allows stronger ones to survive and multiply, potentially developing into multi-drug resistant TB (MDR-TB) or extensively drug-resistant TB (XDR-TB), which are much harder and more expensive to treat.'),
  t(9, '### Cardiovascular Disease (CVD) and STIs\n\n**Cardiovascular disease:**\nCVD includes diseases of the heart and blood vessels (heart attacks, strokes, hypertension):\n- Leading cause of death globally and a major killer in SA\n- Risk factors: high blood pressure, high cholesterol, smoking, obesity, diabetes, physical inactivity, stress\n- Prevention: regular exercise, healthy diet, avoiding smoking, managing stress, regular blood pressure checks\n\n**Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs):**\nSTIs are infections spread through sexual contact:\n\n| STI | Symptoms | Treatment |\n|-----|----------|----------|\n| **Chlamydia** | Often no symptoms; discharge, pain during urination | Antibiotics |\n| **Gonorrhoea** | Discharge, painful urination, pelvic pain | Antibiotics |\n| **Syphilis** | Painless sores, rash, organ damage if untreated | Antibiotics (penicillin) |\n| **Herpes** | Painful blisters, recurring outbreaks | Antiviral medication (no cure) |\n| **HPV** | Genital warts, cervical cancer risk | HPV vaccine for prevention; warts can be treated |\n| **HIV/AIDS** | Weakened immune system, opportunistic infections | Antiretroviral therapy (ART) — no cure but manageable |'),
  t(10, '### HIV/AIDS in South Africa\n\nHIV/AIDS remains the most significant public health challenge in South Africa:\n\n**Key statistics:**\n- Approximately 8 million South Africans are living with HIV\n- SA has the largest antiretroviral therapy (ART) programme in the world\n- New infections have declined but remain high, especially among young women aged 15-24\n\n**Living with HIV:**\n- ART allows people with HIV to live long, healthy lives\n- An undetectable viral load means HIV cannot be transmitted sexually (U=U: Undetectable = Untransmittable)\n- Stigma and discrimination remain barriers to testing and treatment\n- Disclosure is a personal decision protected by the right to privacy\n\n**Support and resources:**\n- Treatment Action Campaign (TAC): advocacy for access to treatment\n- South African National AIDS Council (SANAC): coordinates the national HIV response\n- Free HIV testing and ART at all government clinics\n- National AIDS Helpline: 0800 012 322\n\n**Your role:**\n- Get tested regularly\n- Do not stigmatise or discriminate against people living with HIV\n- Practise safe sexual behaviour\n- Support friends and family who are affected'),
  q(11, 'What does "U=U" (Undetectable = Untransmittable) mean in the context of HIV?',
    ['A person with an undetectable viral load on ART cannot transmit HIV sexually', 'HIV has been cured and is no longer a concern', 'Universal testing means universal treatment', 'Unprotected sex is safe if you feel healthy'], 0,
    'U=U is a scientifically proven concept: when a person living with HIV takes ART consistently and achieves an undetectable viral load, they cannot transmit HIV through sexual contact. This does not mean HIV is cured — ART must be taken for life.'),
  fb(12, 'The combination of TB and ___ is particularly deadly because the latter weakens the immune system. South Africa has the largest ___ therapy programme in the world.',
    ['HIV', 'antiretroviral'],
    'HIV weakens the immune system, making TB infection much more likely to progress to active disease. South Africa runs the world\'s largest ART programme to manage HIV.'),
];

