// CHAPTER 8: Labour Laws and the World of Work (Term 3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Labour Laws and the World of Work\n\nUnderstanding labour laws protects your rights as a worker and helps you navigate the world of work. South Africa has comprehensive labour legislation that governs employment relationships.\n\n### The Job Contract (Employment Contract)\n\nAn employment contract is a legally binding agreement between an employer and an employee:\n\n**What must be included:**\n- Full names of employer and employee\n- Job title and description of duties\n- Place of work\n- Working hours (ordinary hours and overtime arrangements)\n- Remuneration (salary/wages), how it is calculated, and payment frequency\n- Leave entitlements (annual leave, sick leave, family responsibility leave)\n- Notice period for termination\n- Start date and duration (permanent, fixed-term, or temporary)\n\n**Important notes:**\n- Even verbal agreements are legally binding, but written contracts provide better protection\n- An employer must provide a written contract or written particulars within two months of employment starting\n- Never sign a contract without reading and understanding it fully\n- If you do not understand something, ask for clarification or seek legal advice'),
  t(2, '### Workers Rights in South Africa\n\nSouth African workers have extensive rights protected by law:\n\n**Constitutional rights (Bill of Rights):**\n- Section 23: Everyone has the right to fair labour practices\n- The right to form and join a trade union\n- The right to strike\n- The right to fair and safe working conditions\n\n**Key rights under labour legislation:**\n- Right to a written employment contract\n- Right to minimum wage (National Minimum Wage: currently R27.58 per hour)\n- Right to reasonable working hours (max 45 hours/week for most workers)\n- Right to overtime pay (1.5 times normal rate)\n- Right to leave: 21 consecutive days annual leave, 36 days sick leave in a 3-year cycle, 3 days family responsibility leave per year\n- Right to not be unfairly dismissed\n- Right to a safe and healthy workplace\n- Right to equal pay for work of equal value\n- Protection against unfair discrimination in the workplace'),
  q(3, 'Thabo works 50 hours per week and is paid R25 per hour with no overtime. Which of his rights is being violated?',
    ['His right to the national minimum wage and to overtime pay for hours exceeding 45 per week', 'His right to annual leave only', 'No rights are being violated as the employer sets the terms', 'His right to join a trade union'], 0,
    'Thabo is being paid below the National Minimum Wage (R27.58/hour) and is not receiving overtime pay for the 5 hours exceeding the 45-hour weekly maximum. Both violate the Basic Conditions of Employment Act.'),
  t(4, '### The Labour Relations Act (LRA), No. 66 of 1995\n\nThe LRA is the primary law governing labour relations in South Africa:\n\n**Purpose:**\n- Promote economic development, social justice, labour peace, and democratisation of the workplace\n- Regulate the rights of trade unions, employers, and employer organisations\n- Provide a framework for collective bargaining\n- Regulate strikes and lockouts\n- Prevent and resolve labour disputes\n\n**Key provisions:**\n- **Unfair dismissal:** An employer may not dismiss an employee without a fair reason and a fair procedure\n- **Unfair labour practices:** Includes unfair conduct related to promotion, demotion, training, or benefits\n- **Freedom of association:** Workers have the right to form or join trade unions\n- **Collective bargaining:** Unions negotiate wages and conditions on behalf of members\n- **Dispute resolution:** The CCMA (Commission for Conciliation, Mediation and Arbitration) resolves disputes between employers and employees\n\n**The CCMA:**\n- A free service that helps resolve workplace disputes\n- Handles conciliation (trying to reach agreement) and arbitration (making a binding decision)\n- Workers can refer disputes to the CCMA without needing a lawyer\n- Contact: 0861 16 16 16 or visit www.ccma.org.za'),
  fb(5, 'The law that governs labour relations including unfair dismissal and collective bargaining is the ___ Act. The body that resolves workplace disputes for free is the ___.',
    ['Labour Relations', 'CCMA'],
    'The Labour Relations Act (LRA) is the primary labour relations legislation. The CCMA (Commission for Conciliation, Mediation and Arbitration) provides free dispute resolution services.'),
  t(6, '### The Employment Equity Act (EEA), No. 55 of 1998\n\nThe EEA aims to achieve equity in the workplace:\n\n**Purpose:**\n- Promote equal opportunity and fair treatment through elimination of unfair discrimination\n- Implement affirmative action measures to redress disadvantages experienced by designated groups\n\n**Designated groups:**\n- Black people (African, Coloured, Indian)\n- Women\n- People with disabilities\n\n**What the EEA requires:**\n- Employers must eliminate unfair discrimination in employment policies and practices\n- Designated employers (50+ employees or above a certain turnover) must prepare and implement employment equity plans\n- Designated employers must report to the Department of Employment and Labour on their progress\n\n**Important distinction:**\n- Affirmative action is NOT unfair discrimination — it is a legal and constitutional mechanism to correct historical imbalances\n- Section 9(2) of the Constitution explicitly allows for measures to promote equality for previously disadvantaged groups'),
  t(7, '### The Basic Conditions of Employment Act (BCEA), No. 75 of 1997\n\nThe BCEA sets minimum employment conditions:\n\n| Condition | Requirement |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Working hours** | Maximum 45 hours per week (9 hours/day for 5-day week, 8 hours/day for 6-day week) |\n| **Overtime** | Maximum 10 hours/week; paid at 1.5 times normal rate (or 2 times on Sundays/public holidays) |\n| **Annual leave** | 21 consecutive days per year (or 1 day for every 17 days worked) |\n| **Sick leave** | 36 days in a 3-year cycle (equivalent to 6 weeks in first 6 months) |\n| **Family responsibility leave** | 3 days per year (for birth of child, illness of child, death of close family) |\n| **Maternity leave** | 4 consecutive months (UIF pays a portion of salary) |\n| **Notice period** | 1 week (0-6 months), 2 weeks (6-12 months), 4 weeks (1+ year) |\n| **Child labour** | Prohibited under age 15; restricted for ages 15-17 |'),
  q(8, 'Nomsa has worked at a company for 2 years. She is being dismissed with one week\'s notice. Is this lawful?',
    ['No, she is entitled to 4 weeks notice because she has worked for more than one year', 'Yes, one week is always sufficient notice', 'No, she cannot be dismissed at all after 2 years', 'Yes, notice periods are optional in South Africa'], 0,
    'Under the BCEA, an employee who has worked for more than one year is entitled to at least 4 weeks notice. One week notice applies only to employees in their first 6 months of employment.'),
  t(9, '### Trade Unions, Equity and Redress\n\n**Trade unions:**\n- Organisations formed by workers to protect their interests and rights\n- Major SA trade unions: COSATU, FEDUSA, NACTU, SAFTU\n- Functions: negotiate wages, represent members in disputes, advocate for better conditions\n- The right to join a union is protected by Section 23 of the Bill of Rights and the LRA\n- Union members pay monthly subscriptions (union dues)\n\n**Equity and redress in the workplace:**\n- Historical context: Apartheid created massive inequalities in employment opportunities, education, and wealth\n- Redress aims to correct these historical injustices through:\n  - Affirmative action policies\n  - Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (B-BBEE)\n  - Skills development programmes\n  - Bursaries and learnerships targeting previously disadvantaged groups\n- The goal is not to disadvantage any group but to create genuine equality of opportunity'),
  t(10, '### The Recruitment Process\n\nUnderstanding the recruitment process prepares you for entering the job market:\n\n**Steps in the recruitment process:**\n1. **Job analysis:** Employer identifies the need for a position and defines the role\n2. **Job advertisement:** Position is advertised (newspapers, websites, social media)\n3. **Application:** Candidates submit CVs, cover letters, and required documents\n4. **Shortlisting:** Employer selects candidates who meet minimum requirements\n5. **Interviews:** Shortlisted candidates are interviewed (may include tests or assessments)\n6. **Selection:** The most suitable candidate is chosen based on qualifications, experience, and fit\n7. **Appointment:** Successful candidate receives a job offer and signs a contract\n8. **Induction:** New employee is oriented to the workplace, policies, and colleagues\n\n**Your rights during recruitment:**\n- You may not be discriminated against on grounds of race, gender, disability, pregnancy, etc.\n- Interview questions must be relevant to the job\n- You have the right to know the outcome of your application'),
  q(11, 'Which South African law specifically aims to correct historical workplace imbalances through affirmative action?',
    ['The Employment Equity Act (EEA)', 'The Labour Relations Act (LRA)', 'The Basic Conditions of Employment Act (BCEA)', 'The Companies Act'], 0,
    'The Employment Equity Act specifically addresses affirmative action and the elimination of unfair discrimination in the workplace to redress historical imbalances.'),
  t(12, '### Work Ethics\n\nWork ethics are the values and principles that guide your conduct in the workplace:\n\n**Key work ethics:**\n- **Punctuality:** Arriving on time shows respect for others and commitment to your responsibilities\n- **Reliability:** Doing what you say you will do, meeting deadlines, being consistent\n- **Professionalism:** Dressing appropriately, communicating respectfully, maintaining confidentiality\n- **Honesty and integrity:** Being truthful, not stealing time or resources, admitting mistakes\n- **Accountability:** Taking responsibility for your actions and their consequences\n- **Continuous learning:** Being willing to learn new skills and improve\n- **Respect:** Treating colleagues, clients, and superiors with dignity regardless of their position\n\n**Why work ethics matter:**\n- They build your professional reputation\n- They create a positive and productive work environment\n- They lead to career advancement and opportunities\n- They contribute to the success of the organisation\n- In a country with high unemployment, good work ethics help you stand out and keep your job'),
];

