blockNum = 0;
const ch9_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Revision: Grade 11 Life Orientation\n\nThis chapter consolidates the key concepts from all chapters covered during the year. Use this as a study guide to prepare for your assessments.\n\n### Term 1 Revision: Development of Self — Healthy Lifestyle\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Lifestyle diseases (NCDs) are linked to unhealthy behaviours, not infections\n- Major risk factors: unhealthy diet, physical inactivity, smoking, alcohol abuse, unsafe sexual behaviour, stress\n- Major lifestyle diseases: cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, cancer, chronic respiratory diseases\n- Prevention focuses on lifestyle modification: balanced diet, regular exercise, avoiding tobacco and excess alcohol\n- Long-term physical engagement benefits: physical health, mental health, cognitive function, social benefits, disease prevention, self-esteem\n- SA context: dual burden of NCDs and infectious diseases, SADAG support, government clinic chronic medication'),
  t(2, '### Term 1 Revision: Relationships\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Types of relationships: family, friendships, romantic, professional, community\n- Rights in relationships: dignity (Section 10), privacy (Section 14), freedom and security (Section 12), equality (Section 9)\n- Responsibilities: respect, honesty, communication, supporting others, taking accountability\n- Qualities for healthy relationships: trust, respect, communication, empathy, honesty, commitment, support\n- Detrimental relationships: abusive, controlling, toxic — recognise warning signs\n- Legal protection: Domestic Violence Act, Sexual Offences Act, Children\'s Act\n- Appropriate expression of feelings: "I" statements, right time and place, cultural awareness\n- Support: Childline 0800 055 555, Stop GBV 0800 150 150'),
  q(3, 'Which section of the Bill of Rights protects the right to human dignity?',
    ['Section 10', 'Section 9', 'Section 12', 'Section 16'], 0,
    'Section 10 of the Bill of Rights states: "Everyone has inherent dignity and the right to have their dignity respected and protected."'),
  t(4, '### Term 1 Revision: Study Skills\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Learning styles: visual, auditory, reading/writing, kinaesthetic\n- Effective study strategies: active recall, spaced repetition, Pomodoro Technique, elaborative interrogation, interleaving\n- Command words: define, identify, describe, explain, discuss, evaluate, analyse, compare, distinguish\n- Exam writing: read the whole paper first, allocate time by marks, underline key words, answer all questions\n- Study plan: list subjects, check timetable, allocate time blocks, set specific goals, include breaks, review weekly\n- Dealing with challenges: procrastination (break tasks down), motivation (connect to goals), distractions (eliminate), anxiety (prepare and breathe)'),
  t(5, '### Term 1 Revision: Careers and Career Choices\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Competencies: technical skills, soft skills, emotional intelligence, ethical competence\n- Workplace ethics: integrity, accountability, confidentiality, respect, fairness\n- Unethical behaviour: fraud, corruption, nepotism, discrimination, plagiarism\n- CV structure: personal details, personal statement, education, work experience, skills, achievements, references\n- Job interview: research the company, prepare for common questions, dress professionally, arrive early, follow up\n- Career exploration: self-assessment, NCAP, SAQA, DHET, university open days\n- Post-school options: university, UoT, TVET, learnerships, entrepreneurship'),
  fb(6, 'The five main conflict resolution styles are competing, compromising, ___, collaborating, and avoiding. The CCMA stands for Commission for Conciliation, ___ and Arbitration.',
    ['accommodating', 'Mediation'],
    'The five conflict resolution styles are: competing, compromising, accommodating, collaborating, and avoiding. CCMA = Commission for Conciliation, Mediation and Arbitration.'),
  t(7, '### Term 2 Revision: Democracy and Human Rights\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Media functions: informing, watchdog, platform for debate, educating, agenda setting\n- Freedom of expression (Section 16): protected but limited — no propaganda for war, incitement to violence, or hate speech\n- Digital footprint: permanent record of online activity; future employers check\n- Cyberbullying: criminalised under the Cybercrimes Act (2020)\n- Cyber safety: strong passwords, two-factor authentication, think before posting, report and block\n- Discrimination: direct, indirect, hate speech — prohibited by Section 9 and PEPUDA\n- Sports and nation building: 1995 RWC, 2010 FIFA WC, transformation challenges\n- Ideologies: democracy, socialism, capitalism, ubuntu, feminism, conservatism'),
  t(8, '### Term 2 Revision: Social and Environmental Responsibility\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Three levels of government: national (Parliament), provincial (Premier), local (municipalities)\n- Local government provides: water, electricity, refuse removal, sanitation, local roads\n- Social grants administered by SASSA: Child Support, Old Age, Disability, Foster Care\n- Community services: NGOs, faith-based organisations, CPFs, stokvels, volunteering\n- Environmental issues: water scarcity, air pollution, land degradation, waste, climate change, biodiversity loss\n- Environmental programmes: Working for Water, Working on Fire, EPWP, SANParks\n- Section 24: right to a healthy environment\n- Personal mission (what you do now) and vision (where you want to be) statements'),
  q(9, 'Which of the following correctly matches the labour law with its main purpose?',
    ['LRA: labour relations and dispute resolution; EEA: workplace equity and affirmative action; BCEA: minimum employment conditions', 'LRA: minimum wages; EEA: dispute resolution; BCEA: affirmative action', 'LRA: health and safety; EEA: working hours; BCEA: trade union registration', 'All three laws serve the same purpose with different names'], 0,
    'The LRA governs labour relations, unfair dismissal, and dispute resolution. The EEA addresses workplace equity and affirmative action. The BCEA sets minimum conditions of employment like working hours, leave, and notice periods.'),
  t(10, '### Term 3 Revision: Lifestyle Diseases and Health\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Human factors causing ill-health: poor eating, smoking, substance abuse, unsafe sexual behaviour\n- Substances: alcohol (liver disease, FASD), cannabis (memory/motivation), tik (psychosis), nyaope (severe addiction)\n- Cancer: cervical (HPV vaccine), breast, prostate, lung (smoking); prevention through screening and lifestyle\n- TB: high rates in SA, TB-HIV co-infection, complete full course of medication, drug resistance\n- CVD: heart attacks, strokes, hypertension; prevention through exercise, diet, no smoking\n- STIs: chlamydia, gonorrhoea, syphilis, herpes, HPV, HIV/AIDS\n- HIV/AIDS: 8 million living with HIV in SA, ART, U=U, free testing at clinics\n- Support: CANSA 0800 22 66 22, AIDS Helpline 0800 012 322, loveLife'),
  t(11, '### Term 3 Revision: Labour Laws and the World of Work\n\n**Key concepts:**\n- Employment contract: must include job description, hours, salary, leave, notice period\n- Key laws: LRA (labour relations, CCMA), EEA (equity, affirmative action), BCEA (minimum conditions)\n- BCEA conditions: 45 hours/week max, overtime at 1.5x, 21 days annual leave, 36 days sick leave per 3-year cycle\n- National Minimum Wage: R27.58/hour\n- Trade unions: COSATU, FEDUSA, NACTU, SAFTU — right to join protected by Section 23\n- CCMA: free dispute resolution, conciliation and arbitration\n- Recruitment process: job analysis, advertisement, application, shortlisting, interview, selection, appointment, induction\n- Work ethics: punctuality, reliability, professionalism, integrity, accountability, continuous learning, respect'),
  q(12, 'A Grade 11 learner is preparing for their Life Orientation exam. Which approach would BEST help them study effectively?',
    ['Create a study plan with specific goals, use active recall and spaced repetition, and practise past papers', 'Read through all the notes once the night before', 'Only study the topics they find interesting', 'Memorise definitions without understanding the concepts'], 0,
    'Effective studying combines planning (study plan with goals), evidence-based techniques (active recall, spaced repetition), and practice (past papers). Simply reading notes once, selective studying, or rote memorisation without understanding are all ineffective approaches.'),
];

